Chapter: Interval

 The shared interval-arithmetic kernel for the compiler's two range
 provers. The checker's aexpr-proven-range and the emitter's
 ir-expr-proven-range each carry a private, hand-written copy of
 interval arithmetic today; both refuse everything but non-negative
 operands, so a named local holding a provably small signed value has
 no provable range at all. This chapter is the single kernel both
 provers will consume, written for full signed i64 semantics and
 verified by an exhaustive containment test
 (codex/test/interval-exhaustive.codex) in the ConstantRange
 discipline: every transfer function states its soundness contract in
 prose, and the test checks that contract against the runtime's own
 operators for every interval over a small universe. The kernel is
 pure arithmetic over a two-field record; it allocates one record per
 call and nothing else.

 An Interval is the set of signed 64-bit integers { v : lo <= v <= hi }
 with INCLUSIVE endpoints. The full i64 range is the "unknown"
 interval: it is always a sound answer, and every transfer function
 returns it whenever an endpoint computation would overflow i64. The
 kernel proves containment only - no relational facts, no strides, no
 known bits; the full list of what it deliberately does not do is in
 the Limitations section at the end.

Section: Representation

 Two distinguished values. iv-unknown is the full i64 range, the
 sound answer of last resort. iv-empty is the empty interval - the
 witness that no value can flow, produced by intersecting disjoint
 ranges. Empty is represented by lo > hi, tested by iv-is-empty, and
 every function in this chapter that produces an empty result returns
 the one canonical value iv-empty, never any other lo > hi pair.
 Returning iv-unknown for an empty intersection would be sound but
 lossy; the distinguished empty is one comparison and keeps
 dead-branch facts exact. A transfer function handed an empty operand
 returns iv-empty: the soundness contracts below quantify over the
 concrete members of the inputs, and an empty input has none, so the
 empty result is vacuously sound and maximally precise.

  Interval = record {
   lo : Integer,
   hi : Integer
  }

 The i64 endpoint constants. The compiler defines i64-min and i64-max
 in the Codex Types chapter, but this chapter must stand alone for
 consumers that cite only the kernel, and redefining those names here
 would collide in the self-compile unit, so the kernel carries its
 own prefixed copies.

  iv-i64-max : Integer = 9223372036854775807

  iv-i64-min : Integer = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1

  iv-unknown : Interval = Interval { lo = iv-i64-min, hi = iv-i64-max }

  iv-empty : Interval = Interval { lo = 1, hi = 0 }

  iv-is-unknown : Interval -> Boolean
  iv-is-unknown (a) = a.lo == iv-i64-min & a.hi == iv-i64-max

  iv-is-empty : Interval -> Boolean
  iv-is-empty (a) = a.lo > a.hi

  iv-exact : Integer -> Interval
  iv-exact (n) = Interval { lo = n, hi = n }

Section: Overflow Refusal

 Predicates that decide whether an i64 endpoint computation would
 overflow, without themselves overflowing. Addition uses the standard
 rearrangement: a + b overflows iff b > 0 and a > max - b, or b <= 0
 and a < min - b; both right-hand sides stay in range under their
 guards. Subtraction mirrors it. Multiplication compares against a
 truncating division of the endpoint constants - Codex integer
 division truncates toward zero, which is exactly the rounding these
 bounds need - after handling the two corners where either operand is
 -1 against i64-min, because negating i64-min is itself an overflow.

  iv-add-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-add-overflows (a) (b) =
   if b > 0 then a > iv-i64-max - b
   else a < iv-i64-min - b

  iv-sub-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-sub-overflows (a) (b) =
   if b > 0 then a < iv-i64-min + b
   else a > iv-i64-max + b

  iv-mul-overflows : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  iv-mul-overflows (a) (b) =
   if a == 0 | b == 0 then False
   else if a == 0 - 1 then b == iv-i64-min
   else if b == 0 - 1 then a == iv-i64-min
   else if b > 0 then a > iv-i64-max / b | a < iv-i64-min / b
   else a > iv-i64-min / b | a < iv-i64-max / b

 Integer minimum and maximum, used to order corner results below.

  iv-int-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  iv-int-min (a) (b) = if a <= b then a else b

  iv-int-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  iv-int-max (a) (b) = if a >= b then a else b

Section: Addition

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range and y in the other input's range, the concrete result
 x + y lies inside the returned interval. Addition is monotone in
 both arguments, so the exact bounds are the endpoint sums; if either
 endpoint sum would overflow i64, the answer is iv-unknown, which is
 always sound.

  iv-add : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-add (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-add-overflows (a.lo) (b.lo) | iv-add-overflows (a.hi) (b.hi) then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = a.lo + b.lo, hi = a.hi + b.hi }

Section: Subtraction

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range and y in the other input's range, the concrete result
 x - y lies inside the returned interval. Subtraction is monotone up
 in the left argument and monotone down in the right, so the exact
 bounds are lo = a.lo - b.hi and hi = a.hi - b.lo; if either would
 overflow i64, the answer is iv-unknown.

  iv-sub : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-sub (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-sub-overflows (a.lo) (b.hi) | iv-sub-overflows (a.hi) (b.lo) then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = a.lo - b.hi, hi = a.hi - b.lo }

Section: Negation

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete x in the input's range, the
 concrete result 0 - x lies inside the returned interval. Negation
 swaps and negates the endpoints exactly, except that negating
 iv-i64-min overflows, so any interval containing it answers
 iv-unknown.

  iv-neg : Interval -> Interval
  iv-neg (a) =
   if iv-is-empty a then iv-empty
   else if a.lo == iv-i64-min then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = 0 - a.hi, hi = 0 - a.lo }

Section: Multiplication

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range and y in the other input's range, the concrete result
 x * y lies inside the returned interval. For fixed x the product is
 monotone in y (direction given by the sign of x) and symmetrically
 for fixed y, so over the input box the extremes are attained at the
 four corners; the result is the min and max of the four corner
 products. If any corner product would overflow i64, the answer is
 iv-unknown.

  iv-mul : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-mul (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-mul-overflows (a.lo) (b.lo) | iv-mul-overflows (a.lo) (b.hi) | iv-mul-overflows (a.hi) (b.lo) | iv-mul-overflows (a.hi) (b.hi) then iv-unknown
   else let p1 = a.lo * b.lo
   in let p2 = a.lo * b.hi
   in let p3 = a.hi * b.lo
   in let p4 = a.hi * b.hi
   in Interval {
    lo = iv-int-min (iv-int-min p1 p2) (iv-int-min p3 p4),
    hi = iv-int-max (iv-int-max p1 p2) (iv-int-max p3 p4)
   }

Section: Division

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range, y in the other input's range, and y not zero, the
 concrete result x / y lies inside the returned interval. Codex
 division truncates toward zero (idiv), and truncating division is
 monotone in the dividend for a fixed divisor and monotone in the
 divisor over a divisor range of fixed sign, so the extremes over the
 box are attained at the four corners. A divisor range containing
 zero answers iv-unknown: the range still holds nonzero divisors
 whose quotients must be contained, and unknown contains them all.
 The single overflowing corner, iv-i64-min divided by -1, also
 answers iv-unknown; a negative divisor range contains -1 exactly
 when its high endpoint is -1.

  iv-div : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-div (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if b.lo <= 0 & b.hi >= 0 then iv-unknown
   else if a.lo == iv-i64-min & b.hi == 0 - 1 then iv-unknown
   else let q1 = a.lo / b.lo
   in let q2 = a.lo / b.hi
   in let q3 = a.hi / b.lo
   in let q4 = a.hi / b.hi
   in Interval {
    lo = iv-int-min (iv-int-min q1 q2) (iv-int-min q3 q4),
    hi = iv-int-max (iv-int-max q1 q2) (iv-int-max q3 q4)
   }

Section: Modulo

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range, y in the other input's range, and y not zero, the
 concrete result int-mod x y lies inside the returned interval. What
 the runtime actually does (emit-int-mod-builtin in
 Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex): idiv leaves the truncated remainder,
 which carries the sign of the DIVIDEND, and a negative remainder is
 then corrected by adding the absolute value of the divisor. The
 result is therefore Euclidean - always in [0, abs(y) - 1] - for
 every sign combination, negative dividends and negative divisors
 included. The bound is [0, m] where m is the largest abs(y) - 1 over
 the divisor corners; abs(iv-i64-min) - 1 is exactly iv-i64-max, so
 the magnitude is computable at every corner. A divisor range
 containing zero answers iv-unknown, same argument as division.

  iv-mod-mag : Integer -> Integer
  iv-mod-mag (n) =
   if n == iv-i64-min then iv-i64-max
   else if n < 0 then (0 - n) - 1
   else n - 1

  iv-mod : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-mod (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if b.lo <= 0 & b.hi >= 0 then iv-unknown
   else Interval { lo = 0, hi = iv-int-max (iv-mod-mag (b.lo)) (iv-mod-mag (b.hi)) }

Section: Union

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: every concrete x in either input's range lies
 inside the returned interval. The result is the convex hull - the
 gap between disjoint inputs is included, because an interval cannot
 represent it. An empty side contributes nothing and the other side
 is returned unchanged. No overflow is possible: the result endpoints
 are copies of input endpoints.

  iv-union : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-union (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a & iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else if iv-is-empty a then b
   else if iv-is-empty b then a
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-min (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-max (a.hi) (b.hi) }

Section: Intersection

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: every concrete x that lies in BOTH inputs'
 ranges lies inside the returned interval; the returned interval is
 in fact exactly the set of such x. Disjoint inputs produce the
 canonical iv-empty. An empty input needs no special case: taking the
 max of the lows and the min of the highs against any lo > hi pair
 yields lo > hi again, which normalizes to iv-empty.

  iv-intersect : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-intersect (a) (b) =
   let n-lo = iv-int-max (a.lo) (b.lo)
   in let n-hi = iv-int-min (a.hi) (b.hi)
   in if n-lo > n-hi then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = n-lo, hi = n-hi }

Section: Minimum and Maximum

 SOUNDNESS CONTRACT: for every concrete pair (x, y) with x in the
 input's range and y in the other input's range, the concrete result
 min(x, y) lies inside the returned interval - and symmetrically for
 max. min(x, y) is at least the smaller lo and at most the smaller hi
 (it is bounded above by both x and y separately); max mirrors the
 argument with the larger endpoints. Both are exact and cannot
 overflow.

  iv-min : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-min (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-min (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-min (a.hi) (b.hi) }

  iv-max : Interval, Interval -> Interval
  iv-max (a) (b) =
   if iv-is-empty a | iv-is-empty b then iv-empty
   else Interval { lo = iv-int-max (a.lo) (b.lo), hi = iv-int-max (a.hi) (b.hi) }

Section: Limitations

 What this kernel provably does not do, so nobody rediscovers the
 gaps the hard way.

 It carries no relational facts: it cannot express i != j, x < y, or
 any link between two expressions - each interval is an independent
 fact about one value. It carries no stride or congruence information
 (an interval of even numbers is indistinguishable from its hull) and
 no known-bits domain. It refuses to iv-unknown whenever ANY endpoint
 computation would overflow i64; there are no wrapping intervals, so
 a sum that overflows loses everything, not just the overflowing end.
 Division and modulo refuse the whole answer when the divisor range
 merely CONTAINS zero - no splitting of a mixed-sign divisor range
 around zero is attempted. The modulo bound is magnitude-only,
 [0, max abs(divisor) - 1]: it does not exploit the dividend at all,
 so int-mod of a small non-negative dividend by a large divisor is
 not recognized as the dividend itself. Union returns the convex
 hull, losing any gap between disjoint inputs. And the empty interval
 is a single canonical witness, not a representation of arbitrary
 empty sets: consumers must test with iv-is-empty, never compare
 endpoints of an empty result.

